# `round` is spelled three ways in one run and the interpreter sees two of
# them. `import "std/math"` enrols a bare clone, and by the time a body is
# evaluated that clone is `sample/round`; the builtin underneath it is
# `builtin_round`; and `shade`'s parameter is the bare `round`, which is a
# binding and never reaches name resolution at all.
#
# That separation is why the interpreter may remember what a non-local name
# stands for: no spelling it remembers is a spelling a binding can take. What
# this fixture pins is the separation itself, not the memory — a memory
# consulted in the wrong order would still print these three numbers, which
# was found by building one and watching it pass.
import "std/math"

fn apply f x
  f x

fn shade round
  round * 10

pub play = print "{shade 4} {apply round 2.6} {shade 6}"
